-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Hunter Henry</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 PIT are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.39 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to NE \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Rhamondre Stevenson</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>NE \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>NE are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>39%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 4-point favorite projected to lead for 33% of snaps, <strong>NE</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Rhamondre Stevenson</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>14 carries for 59 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as NE salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>A.J. Brown</strong> (8 targets, 68 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when NE lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Hunter Henry</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 34 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>PIT \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>PIT are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 PIT project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Jaylen Warren</strong>'s rushing projection (12 carries, 58 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>DK Metcalf</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, PIT funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>7 targets for 60 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Pat Freiermuth</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 32 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 4-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"PIT","col":"#FFD84D","players":[{"name":"Aaron Rodgers","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":227.96,"book":null,"over_th":215.56,"under_th":240.15,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.75,"book":null,"over_th":1.05,"under_th":1.44,"over_am":null,"under_a